o disappear when they move off the right edge of the board.\n- Goal model: - Remove all red blocks from the board (level clears when no reds remain). Open question: - How many right\u2011shifts are needed for the rightmost block (ID 7) to exit the board?\n- Recent findings: - Activating a red block that is already within the N columns (e.g., block 6) moves it further right (to become block 7) and creates a new block at a new position; the block does not disappear automatically. - The click\u2011SPACE cycle appears to shift a block horizontally to the right by about 6 columns each activation, and may also generate a new block further right. Open question: - How to make a red block disappear? Perhaps a block must be moved *out* of the N area onto a specific location or perhaps a block must be moved *onto* a different colored region (e.g., the dark\u2011gray or white background) to vanish.\n- Open questions: - The cycle appears to shift blocks horizontally to the right in steps of 4\u20115 columns after each activation, while also possibly creating new blocks. - Continuing the same activation on the right\u2011most red block may eventually bring one into the N columns.\n- Plan: - Repeatedly activate the rightmost red block (currently ID 7 at row 30, col 51) with MOUSE \u2192 SPACE until the red count drops, indicating a block has left the board. We'll loop up to 5 times, stopping early if reds disappear. Now execute loop.\n- Revise any item above immediately if `current_frame` or `history` contradicts it.\nend of world model. \nFocus on what changed most recently in `history`, update the target environment change if needed, and separate gameplay-object changes from HUD-only changes.\nWhen ready, call `action(actions)` from inside the `python` tool with the best valid action or ordered batch selected by your code. If your code has found a reliable short sequence, prefer batching it in one call.\nYou may call `action(actions)` more than once in one Python snippet if your